    <section>
      <h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Where is Logan?</em> is a console-based, narrative-driven mystery text adventure written in C++. Players explore the empty home of a missing friend named Logan, uncover environmental clues, solve a lock-and-key puzzle, and piece together the truth behind his disappearance before escaping the property.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Project Objectives</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Interactive Storytelling:</strong> Deliver an atmospheric mystery through descriptive room exploration and progressive clue discovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Data-Driven Architecture:</strong> Implement scalable, clean C++ data structures (enums, parallel arrays, and a 2D adjacency matrix) to separate game data from core loop logic.</li>
        <li><strong>State-Based Progression:</strong> Manage player inventory, container states, and win conditions dynamically using boolean flags.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. Narrative &amp; Gameplay Loop</h2>
      <h3>Premise</h3>
      <p>
        The player arrives at Logan’s house to find it eerily empty—his dog is missing, a mug sits abandoned on the counter, and his dusty car remains parked outside.
      </p>

      <h3>Core Mechanics &amp; Progression</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Navigation:</strong> Players traverse five interconnected locations (<code>Entrance</code>, <code>Kitchen</code>, <code>Garage</code>, <code>Bedroom</code>, and <code>Outside</code>) using cardinal direction commands (<code>north</code>/<code>n</code>, <code>east</code>/<code>e</code>, <code>south</code>/<code>s</code>, <code>west</code>/<code>w</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>Investigation (<code>search</code>):</strong> Players inspect rooms to discover hidden items.</li>
        <li><strong>Puzzle Solving (<code>open</code>):</strong>
          <ul>
            <li>Finding the <strong>Note</strong> in the <code>Kitchen</code> reveals the existence of a locked box in the <code>Bedroom</code>.</li>
            <li>Finding the <strong>Box Key</strong> in the <code>Garage</code> and the <strong>Box</strong> in the <code>Bedroom</code> allows the player to unlock the container.</li>
            <li>Opening the box reveals a hidden warning (<strong>Note inside the box</strong>) implicating a character named <em>Talan</em> and urging the player to run.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>Win Condition:</strong> Exiting to the <code>Outside</code> area while possessing the final note triggers the endgame sequence.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Technical Architecture</h2>
      <p>The application relies on procedural C++ design patterns tailored for readability and easy expansion:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Component</th>
            <th>Implementation</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Identifiers</strong></td>
            <td><code>enum Direction</code>, <code>Room</code>, <code>Item</code></td>
            <td>Replaces magic numbers with readable constants and auto-tracks array bounds (<code>NUM_ROOMS</code>, <code>NUM_ITEMS</code>).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>World Map</strong></td>
            <td><code>int connections[NUM_ROOMS][NUM_DIRECTIONS]</code></td>
            <td>A 2D adjacency matrix mapping room exits (<code>-1</code> denotes a blocked path).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Entity Data</strong></td>
            <td>Parallel Arrays (<code>roomNames</code>, <code>itemRooms</code>, <code>itemFound</code>)</td>
            <td>Links names, descriptions, locations, and discovery states across matching indices.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Game Engine</strong></td>
            <td><code>while (gameRunning)</code> loop</td>
            <td>Handles turn-based rendering, <code>cin</code> command parsing, state validation, and win-condition checks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Modularity</strong></td>
            <td><code>searchRoom()</code> helper function</td>
            <td>Encapsulates room-searching logic outside <code>main()</code> to keep input handling clean.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5. Future Scope &amp; Planned Enhancements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Improved Entrance &amp; Player Onboarding:</strong> Redesign the opening sequence at the <code>Entrance</code> to establish the story hook more clearly and display a clean menu of known commands (<code>north</code>/<code>n</code>, <code>east</code>/<code>e</code>, <code>south</code>/<code>s</code>, <code>west</code>/<code>w</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>open</code>, <code>quit</code>/<code>q</code>) so first-time players immediately know how to interact with the world.</li>
        <li><strong>Map Expansion (New Room):</strong> Expand the house layout by adding a new room (such as utilizing the commented-out <code>ATTIC</code> placeholder in the <code>Room</code> enum) along with updated parallel array entries and two-way exits in the <code>connections</code> matrix to deepen exploration.</li>
      </ul>
